From: Yusheng Zheng <yunwei356@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 04:19:34 +0000
Subject: [RFC PATCH bpf-next v1 0/5] bpf: Add verified kinsn support

Ordinary kfunc calls cross a call boundary even when an operation can be
expressed in a few machine instructions. The BPF JIT translates a verified
instruction stream in one pass and cannot generally select target-specific
idioms from a longer BPF sequence. Let a registered kernel descriptor
supply a short BPF proof and a bounded native emitter for such operations.
The related paper calls this interface Kinsn; the kernel interface here is
named kinsn.

A program requests a kinsn through a kfunc call preceded by a sidecar MOV
instruction with a 52-bit payload. Registration binds the descriptor to an
explicit kfunc BTF ID and retains the ordinary kfunc allow policy. The
verifier substitutes the descriptor's proof sequence, checks its control
flow and the lowered program, then restores the sidecar and call. The JIT
emits native bytes on x86-64 or arm64 and preserves registers used only by
the proof. A weak bpf_jit_supports_kinsn() defaults to false so unsupported
JITs reject the request.

Only kernel code can register a descriptor. The descriptor owner must show
that each native emitter has the same observable behavior as its verified
proof for every accepted payload. The verifier checks the proof, not that
equivalence. Reviewers' feedback is particularly welcome on the descriptor
API shape, how to review and test native emitter equivalence, and the new
MOV src_reg sidecar encoding in relation to the standardized BPF ISA.

The series' selftests passed on x86-64 KVM and arm64 TCG (Cortex-A76) at
bpf_jit_harden=0 and 2: test_progs -t kinsn passed 23/23 subtests,
including the NETFILTER-only kfunc filter rejection, and
kinsn_registration loaded a dedicated module to check that a descriptor
with KF_SLEEPABLE is rejected at registration. The module is separate
from bpf_testmod so a failed negative assertion cannot block unrelated
tests. With the BPF CI rootfs and selftests kernel config, the full
test_progs run reported 801/7,175 passed, 7,644 skipped, and 3/3 failed
on the official tip; the series reported 803/7,198 passed, 7,948 skipped,
and the same 3/3 failed. There were zero new failing test groups. The
shared failures were d_path, map_kptr_race, and tracing_struct; none is
on the generic BPF CI denylist.

Six operation families were exercised on both architectures. Inline JIT
images contained no call instruction, and native results matched the BPF
proof over 100,000 random inputs and eight edge cases per family on each
architecture. The arm64 path also executed natively under TCG. Four
intentionally wrong emitters for bulk copy, extraction, endian conversion,
and prefetch were caught on both architectures. Prefetch preserved the
observed 64-byte memory contents and program results.

Performance was measured on x86-64 KVM with QEMU and vCPUs pinned to host
P-cores. Every program performed at least 1,024 operations per run. After
a 1,000-repeat warmup, each variant had ten trials of one million repeats
of BPF_PROG_TEST_RUN. Times below are median [min,max] ns per operation.
Changing predicates used a fresh random seed per run. The ordinary-
kfunc JIT image has one real call per iteration of the 1,024-iteration
loop, so its throughput timing includes call and return.

Each row is ordered pure BPF, kinsn, ordinary kfunc.

