The throughput shape uses independent operations. Kinsn speeds up rotate
and changing-predicate select against pure BPF. The ordinary kfunc ties
kinsn for rotate and is about 2.5% slower for changing select. Predictable
select, extraction, and endian conversion are near ties. Bulk copy at 16,
32, 64, 128, and 256 bytes also tied pure BPF within about 1%. On random
64-MiB BPF array lookups, prefetch measured 9.828/10.060 for pure
BPF/kinsn, a 2.3% kinsn loss. The hint immediately precedes the load
and has no useful lead time. Bulk copy and prefetch have no ordinary
kfunc comparison because pointer and memory effects differ.

In the dependent-chain shape, each operation consumes the preceding
result; that table comes from a single boot. Kinsn is faster than both
alternatives for rotate and changing select, and ties for extraction and
endian conversion. Across three boots, pure-BPF changing select ranged
from 3.36 to 3.79 ns per operation and kinsn from 1.81 to 1.86.
Predictable select is a clear kinsn loss, about 2x slower than pure BPF.
With a verifier-known constant predicate (const pred), the verifier
removes the dead pure-BPF branch, which a kinsn cannot benefit from. With
a run-invariant predicate the verifier cannot know (run pred), the
correctly predicted pure-BPF branch is still faster than the kinsn
conditional move. The ordinary kfunc is within about 10% of kinsn in both
predictable cases. No shape was tuned to select favorable numbers; the
run-invariant row was added to separate verifier constant folding from
branch prediction.

The changing-select verifier proof also has a load-time cost. In the
throughput shape, pure BPF and kinsn each loaded in about 8.8 ms,
compared with about 1.9 ms for an ordinary kfunc. The dependent chain
loaded in about 12.6 ms for pure BPF and kinsn versus 2.2 ms for the
kfunc. Host-side cpu_core counters on pinned QEMU vCPU threads counted
4.95 billion branch misses in 229 billion instructions for pure BPF and
10.2 million in 215 billion instructions for kinsn across steady changing-
select intervals. The counters include other vCPU work, but the difference
supports branch avoidance as the cause of the changing-select speedup.
The guest vPMU did not expose a branch-miss event. Arm64 TCG was used for
functional validation only; no arm64 timing is claimed. The out-of-tree
benchmark module and loader are available on request.

The related paper is available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.24213.

